\startchapter[reference=methods, title=Methods]

\startsection[title={Dimensionality Reduction Methods}]

Dimensionality reduction methods embed high dimensional data into a low-dimensional space. The intrinsic dimensionality of transcriptomic data is low enough that a 2 or 3 dimensional embedding is able to faithfully reflect structures in the data \cite[ding2018].

\startsubsection[title={Spectral decomposition}]

Spectral decomposition is the act of transforming the data into components that each capture a different source of variance in the data. The components are ranked by their ability to explain the data’s structure. They can be used as dimensionality reduction methods by discarding components with low enough scores. This is most useful as a preprocessing step to define a less noisy latent space reflecting the inherent dimensionality of the data, but components can also be directly visualized.

\startsubsubsection[title={Principal Component Analysis}]
While the intrinsic dimensionality of the data is low, more complex datasets cannot be faithfully represented by a low number of (linear) principal components \cite[lenz2016].
\stopsubsubsection

\startsubsubsection[title={Diffusion Maps}]
Diffusion maps are another spectral decomposition of data. Their practicality for data analysis was first shown by \inlinecite[coifman2005]. A diffusion map preserves global structures and the pseudotemporal ordering of cells better than t-SNE \cite[haghverdi2015], and the first diffusion components are often sufficient to directly capture and visualize bifurcation events in differentiating cells.

\cite[haghverdi2016]
\cite[angerer2016]

